10. ROUND 5 (5 October 2026): THE INDEPENDENT CHECK V6's ITEM C AND V6-B3; T10-A1's ROW, F13, F16 AND F17 WITH THEIR RESPONSES
   V6 (an AI review of candidate 7a82e82a) read T10 CONFIRMED AS CONDITIONAL, not on T10-A1 alone: F13 needs a transmit-share row, F16
   and F17 fault responses traced to CON-004, and the case row's supervisor figure revised; round 4's sentence that placed both fault
   states outside every requirement was wrong (V6-B3). Labels as above; every junction is a MODEL figure, never a measurement

   10a. THE REQUIREMENT, READ WHERE IT IS WRITTEN (V6-B3; round 4 read REQ-073 and REQ-004 and missed it)
   CON-004 (REQUIREMENTS-TRACE.md; constraint, core, BLOCKER): 'Each I/O supervisor has its own regulator branch, reset supervisor, watchdog, crystal and SWD pads, and the three talk over two independent CAN-FD fabrics with separate transceivers and termination.'
     accepted when: 'Netlist shows three independent supply branches and two fabrics; A7 (either fabric cut, the quorum holds) passes.'; allocated to b, fw_ioctrl
   ARCH-PCB-B-IOHA.md section 12 ('Every row is a failure this design is supposed to survive or is knowingly exposed to'):
     row 7 'One CAN fabric breaks or a transceiver fails dominant': quorum continues on the other fabric; detected by bus-off and error counters on the broken fabric
     row 8 'Both CAN fabrics break': no controller can form a majority; firmware rule is that a controller without quorum does not act, so the voters hold the home assignment; detected by each controller sees both fabrics silent
   section 13, test A7 'Heartbeat fabric break': method 'cut fabric A, then fabric B, one at a time'; pass: the quorum kept through each cut (paraphrased: the source's verb is a word this
     record does not print), 'the broken fabric reports bus-off', 'with both broken nothing moves'
   So F16 (one fabric faulted, row 7) and F17 (both, row 8) are inside a mandatory core constraint: their closure is REQUIRED for
   CON-004. A7 as written CUTS a fabric (the 0 Ohm break links R508 to R513): it exercises an open, not a shorted bus, and not the
   TCAN334's 180 mA bus-fault row (TXD 0 V, CANH -12 V, RL open); the shorted-bus variants are analysed in 10e and named in V-B21
   CON-017: '(5) the fitted supervisors are silicon revision V or X'; '(4) the supervisor firmware builds for the STM32H743 and refuses to run both FDCAN fabrics on revision Y or W' (V6-m9). So L9T5-F14's two revisions are
     settled at assembly: the fitted part is V or X. DS12110 Rev 10 prints rev Y (section 6) and rev V (section 7) only, no rev X
     section; T10 keeps rev Y's larger rows as the cover for a rev X part (CONSERVATIVE for rev V by construction, an ASSUMPTION for
     rev X), and rev V's rows as the fitted revision's own figures

   10b. THE REFERENCE MANUAL IS HELD (RM0433 Rev 8, v2/vendor/st/st-rm0433-rev8.pdf, filed 27 September 2026; round 4 took it as not held)
   p.279: 'After reset, the system starts on the lowest Run mode voltage scaling (VOS3)'; p.349: 'After a system reset, the HSI is selected as system clock and all PLLs are switched OFF';
   DS12110 Rev 10: fHSI 64 MHz. So T10-A1's ASSUMPTION is replaced by the printed reset state, HSI at 64 MHz and VOS3: INSIDE the bound
   (VOS3, at most 144 MHz), and the bound stays the least printed row over it
   p.2464: '...or by going Bus_Off. While INIT bit in FDCAN_CCCR register is set, message transfer from and to the CAN bus is stopped, the status of the CAN bus output FDCAN_TX is recessive (high)'
   p.2534: 'If the device goes Bus_Off, it sets FDCAN_CCCR.INIT of its own, stopping all bus activities. Once FDCAN_CCCR.INIT has been cleared by the CPU, the device waits for 129 occurrences of bus Idle' (no automatic recovery)
   p.2470: '...the automatic retransmission may be disabled via FDCAN_CCCR.DAR'; p.2527: FDCAN_CCCR bit 6 DAR. With DAR = 1 a frame is sent once and
     never repeated by the controller, so every transmission is one the firmware scheduled

